    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "With the goal of maintaining insurance coverage during the COVID-19 pandemic, lawmakers allowed Medicaid beneficiaries to be continually eligible for coverage, even if they would normally become disqualified. The policy constituted a major transition in Medicaid policy and changed the budget constraint faced by beneficiaries. In this thesis, I outline the modifications that this policy has to the budget constraint under the static labor model. I then use the Current Population Survey to assess the effects of the beginning of the terminations process on beneficiaries’ labor behavior. I find that the beginning of the terminations process from continuous eligibility increased the number of hours worked for non-parent beneficiaries from between 4.988 and 6.122 hours. These results suggest that non-parent adults respond in a way that counters the static economic model. In contrast, I find no labor effect of terminations on parents, which may result from continued coverage under the Transitional Medical Assistance (TMA) program.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Humphries, D. (2024). Evaluating the Labor-Market Effects of the COVID-19 Medicaid Continuous Enrollment Provision. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/xn499fm8770. https://doi.org/10.25740/xn499fm8770.",
